jordan: FYI customer reported JSON mode outputs that fail our pydantic step. They say it used to validate fine but started breaking after a model update.

sam: can you paste repro and the exact validator error? small snippets are fine.

jordan: here's the curl they used and the raw response we got back (redacted api key):

curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate -H 'Authorization: Bearer REDACTED' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"model":"rwd-3b-structured","json_mode":true,"input":"generate config json for widget"}'

Raw response body:
```
{"result": {"name": "widget-42", "params": {"size": "M",}, "notes": "line1
line2
example"}}
```

jordan: pydantic error: "Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 28 (char 27)" — looks like trailing comma after "size": "M",. Customer sees this intermittently.

sam: trailing comma would definitely break strict json parsers. weird it was intermittent. any difference in model variant or temperature?

jordan: they pinned model to rwd-3b-structured@v2.0. temp=0.2. They said it worked yesterday, started failing ~12 hours ago. I also tried reproducing locally and saw a similar trailing-comma case about 1/6 runs.

rebecca: this smells like schema drift in the model's generation pattern. With structured outputs the LM sometimes emits extra commas or unescaped newlines inside strings. jsonschema would reject too; pydantic can sometimes coerce if not strict, but here parse fails first.

sam: quick triage: add a small normalization pass on our side that tries to extract the first balanced JSON object from the output and then run a couple of fixes: remove trailing commas, replace unescaped newlines within strings with \n, unquote single quotes. can you paste a safe example function?

jordan: here's the snippet I tested in support: 
```js
function sanitizeLMJson(s){
  // naive: extract between first { and matching }
  const start = s.indexOf('{');
  if(start<0) return null;
  let depth=0;
  for(let i=start;i<s.length;i++){
    if(s[i]==='{') depth++;
    if(s[i]==='}') depth--;
    if(depth===0) return s.slice(start,i+1)
  }
  return null;
}
// then: remove trailing commas before } or ], and replace literal newlines in strings with \n

}
```

rebecca: acceptable as short-term mitigation. longer term we should have model-side safety: add a structured-output prompt token that asks model to strictly obey jsonschema and validate before emitting. I can propose a quick change: add an internal 'post_gen_validator' flag that runs jsonschema on the result and if invalid returns a regeneration request with 'regenerate_with_constraints': true.

sam: note difference between validators: jsonschema vs pydantic: jsonschema will tell you precisely which path failed, pydantic will map to model fields. we should standardize the error we send back to customers. right now the support surface is noisy.

incidents-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-1847 assigned to eng-ml. Priority: P2.

jordan: tagging @sam @rebecca — customer wants repro steps so they can continue. I've attached the failing sample and runs count in the ticket.

sam: i'll add an internal experiment: run 100 generations with v2.0 at temp 0.2 and log frequency of trailing commas/unescaped newlines. will post results in the ticket by EOD.

rebecca: also adding note: check if quantization or kernel changes could affect tokenization spacing — might shift comma placement. linking to infra change from last deploy.

jordan: thanks — temporary workaround communicated to customer: post-process sanitizeLMJson then try pydantic.parse_raw. will keep them informed. :thumbsup:
